      {
        "name": "submit_diagnosis",
        "arguments": {
          "service": "frontend",
          "root_cause": "A faulty commit changed frontend.tag from \"v0.10.1\" to the non-existent \"v0.10.99\". Kubernetes cannot pull the image gcr.io/google-samples/microservices-demo/frontend:v0.10.99 (not found in GCR), causing the new frontend pod to stay in ImagePullBackOff. The old replica (v0.10.1) is still serving traffic, but the rollout is broken.",
          "fix": [
            {
              "service": "frontend",
              "key": "tag",
              "value": "v0.10.1"
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ],
    "text": "The root cause is crystal clear. Here's what the evidence shows:\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udd0d Diagnosis\n\n### Root Cause\nThe `frontend` pod is stuck in `ImagePullBackOff` because **its image tag was set to a non-existent version `v0.10.99`**.\n\nThe kubelet error is unambiguous:\n```\nFailed to pull image \"gcr.io/google-samples/microservices-demo/frontend:v0.10.99\": not found\n```\n\n### Evidence Trail\n| Source | Finding |\n|--------|---------|\n| `get_pods` | `frontend-6f44fc5759-w5d4h` \u2192 `0/1 ImagePullBackOff` (new pod); old `frontend-86c6d7449d-c5tdj` still running on old tag |\n| `describe_pod` | Tag `v0.10.99` does not exist in GCR; failed 5\u00d7 in ~3 minutes |\n| `git_recent_changes` | Most recent commit `79083a93fc70` set `frontend.tag` to `v0.10.1` as the **baseline**, but the new failing pod is using `v0.10.99` \u2014 meaning a **subsequent commit** (not shown) bumped the tag to this bogus value |\n\nThe correct tag for all other services in this environment is **`v0.10.1`**, which is what the git baseline shows and what the currently-healthy `frontend` replica is running.\n\n---",
    "tokens": 5073,
    "cost_usd": 0.022071
  }
